<!-- ===== FAQs ===== -->
<section class="px-block soft" id="faqs">
  <div class="ht-wrap">
    <div class="px-sec-head px-reveal">
      <div class="px-kicker">FAQs</div>
      <h2>Food processing equipment questions.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="px-faq">
<details><summary>What food-processing equipment does Harris Thermal fabricate?</summary><div class="a"><p>Custom fry oil heaters, blanching heat exchangers, oil cooling and filtration support equipment within our fabrication scope, falling-film evaporators, crystallizers, condensers and heat recovery equipment, ASME Section VIII vessels and tanks, steam-jacketed and mixing vessels, scrubbers, cyclones and process columns.</p></div></details>
<details><summary>What makes a custom fry oil heater different from a standard heat exchanger?</summary><div class="a"><p>Fry oil heating is a thermal-control problem as much as a heat-duty problem. Oil degrades with time at temperature, so the design has to deliver the required duty without local overheating at the tube wall, while staying cleanable and serviceable in a plant that runs continuously. That pushes the design toward specific tube geometry, velocity and surface-temperature targets rather than a catalog selection. Harris Thermal has extensive experience designing and fabricating custom fry oil heat exchangers for large-scale potato and fried-food processing applications.</p></div></details>
<details><summary>Can Harris Thermal replace or replicate an existing fry oil heater?</summary><div class="a"><p>Yes. Replacement units are built to the geometry already in the plant, so they land on the supports, nozzle positions and piping that are there — working from existing drawings, nameplate data and field measurements, or by reverse-engineering the unit where drawings no longer exist. Where the shell is sound, re-tubing the existing unit is often the better answer, and a unit can be re-rated where the duty has moved past its original basis.</p></div></details>
<details><summary>What materials can Harris Thermal fabricate for food-processing equipment?</summary><div class="a"><p>304L and 316L stainless steel cover most food-processing duty, with carbon steel on utility and structural service and duplex or high-nickel alloys where the stream, temperature or process conditions call for them. Equipment is built to ASME Section VIII, Division 1 and to TEMA requirements where the exchanger design calls for it. Material selection follows the process conditions the project specifies — Harris Thermal fabricates to that specification rather than setting it.</p></div></details>
<details><summary>Can Harris Thermal fabricate evaporators and other process equipment for food and beverage applications?</summary><div class="a"><p>Yes. Falling-film evaporators for concentrating starches, sugars, juices and other liquid food streams, along with the crystallizers, condensers and heat recovery equipment that run with them, plus ASME Section VIII vessels and tanks, steam-jacketed and mixing vessels, scrubbers, cyclones and process columns. Equipment is fabricated to the process conditions, materials and code requirements the project specifies.</p></div></details>
<details><summary>What information is needed to evaluate a food-processing heat exchanger or replacement project?</summary><div class="a"><p>The fluids on both sides and the duty required, the operating temperatures and pressures, and which service the unit is in — fry oil, blanching, evaporation and heat recovery each drive different design choices. For fry oil the maximum acceptable oil-side surface temperature matters as much as the duty, because that is what governs oil degradation. For a replacement, existing drawings or nameplate data, nozzle sizes and positions, and the space and supports the unit has to fit back into are usually enough to start.</p></div></details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

